# Mutation testing per package: <package> <efficacy floor %> <killed mutants floor>.
#
# The ratchet is the KILLED COUNT, because it is the number that cannot fall without an assertion
# being lost. Efficacy is killed over reachable mutants, so covering new code brings new mutants
# into the denominator and *lowers* the ratio while nothing gets worse — control has done that
# twice, going 84.56% -> 84.42% on one pass and 88.54% -> 86.23% on the next, with killed rising
# 126 -> 130 -> 139 -> 144 each time. A floor on the ratio alone would be red for the right work.
#
# So: the efficacy floor may sit below the highest observation and tolerates that dip; the killed
# floor may only move up, and it is checked separately.
#
# Measured 2026-10-27; the sandbox rows were re-measured 2026-11-10 after the driver split.
#
# The split is a move, not a loss: the parent's 389 was one sweep over the shared package and its
# driver subdirectories because gremlins walks a tree. Each row below is one of those packages
# with its own tests; 231 + 80 + 112 + 172 = 595, and the four sweeps run in parallel.
internal/control     88  347
internal/sandbox      100  231
internal/sandbox/bwrap 100 80
internal/sandbox/microsandbox 89 112
internal/sandbox/youki 100  172
internal/supervise   85   47
internal/update      90  258
internal/vault       95  117
internal/workerpool  87    7
internal/ready       97   40
internal/identity    98   69
internal/people      98   66
#
# What a survivor means, read from control's last sweep (169 killed, 10 lived):
#   six are log-only conditions — auth.go:32, auth.go:35, turn.go:269, workspaces.go:1006,
#     workspaces.go:1040 twice. Each one decides whether to write a line, and killing it means
#     asserting on a log string, which pins wording instead of behaviour;
#   three are boundaries on a key that cannot repeat — workspaces.go:316 and :983 sort workspace
#     names, models.go:578 compares scale ranks, and both are unique by construction, so `<` and
#     `<=` are the same function;
#   one is the two-minute budget on turn.go:267, which is only observable by waiting two minutes.
#
# control's ratio fell from 94.41% to 90.83% on the way to a higher killed count (169 -> 218), and that
# is the shape of testing error paths: 58 mutants that were not covered became testable. The first
# pass at those tests executed the code without asserting what it sent, which is why 22 of them lived
# for a while; asserting the commands, the order, the args and the environment took it back up.
#
# sandbox's floor is the count a sweep reads on a machine that boots no engine: its live tests
# ask for ACORND_LIVE_SANDBOX, which `make test-sandbox` sets and a mutation sweep does not, so a
# real boot never lands inside a mutant's test pass — sixteen seconds times four hundred mutants.
# The count used to depend on whether the runner image happened to ship bubblewrap, and when the
# 26.04 image started shipping it the nested-microVM test woke up in the weekly sweep and aborted
# on a /dev/kvm nothing there had made readable. A number that moves with an image is not a
# property of these tests; the switch is what makes it one.
#
# supervise's eight are timing: the idle check's clamps are `interval < 100ms` and `interval >
# 30s`, and `<=`/`>=` produce the same value at the boundary, because the clamp assigns the same
# 100ms and 30s the comparison just tested. Its efficacy reads 85.45% against a floor that was 87
# because the extraction that made those clamps readable brought four more mutants into the
# denominator with the same tests; killed stayed 47, which is the number that matters.
#
# workerpool's one is a boundary that cannot be reached: `len(w.stderr) > stderrKept` and `>=` are
# the same function, because the slice taken at exactly the cap is the whole slice.
#
# vault's four are the same shape: two sort comparators on secret names (vault.go:178, :180, and
# :637 twice), and a name is unique in a list of secrets by definition, so `<` and `<=` are one
# function. Its fifth is a time-out: the mutation makes the store keep its lock, and gremlins
# reports that as timed out rather than killed.
# Those are the honest end of a mutation score, not a gap in the tests.
#
# Not measured, on purpose: `internal/httpapi` and `cmd/acornd` keep their tests in the
# end-to-end suite, and gremlins only runs the package's own. A number from them would report the
# absence of unit tests, not the strength of the tests. Their signal is coverage through the
# feature suite, and `make cover` says it.
#
# microsandbox's thirteen survivors are the machine's own edges, not a gap: kvm_linux.go:25/31/35
# read the KVM group and its name, microsandbox.go:90 is the default state folder, :138/:172 are
# install steps that refuse, :151 a client left unset, :235 a platform without Linux ownership,
# :377 the state-path boundary, microsandbox_secrets.go:310 a secret removal that fails, and
# nested_linux.go:27 a missing KVM parameter file. Each needs a seam that would exist only to be
# broken.
